('browser still owns lost context \u00b7 waiting');return}stopRendering();renderer.dispose();renderer=makeRenderer();positionAttr.needsUpdate=true;normalAttr.needsUpdate=true;colorAttr.needsUpdate=true;rockTexture.needsUpdate=true;resize();document.getElementById('fault').style.display='none';graphicsStatus('renderer rebuilt');startRendering()}catch(e){fail('manual-recover',e)}};try{rebuild();buildWonderField();wonderRoot.visible=false;buildSurfaceCivilization();civRoot.visible=false;seaVolume.visible=false;resize();graphicsStatus('ready \u00b7 inheritance pass 1 \u00b7 flat works \u00b7 water/cities/wonders hidden \u00b7 canonical payloads untouched');startRendering()}catch(e){fail('boot',e)}\n\u003c/script\u003e\n\u003cscript type=\"text/plain\" id=\"works-e8-city-machine-archaeology\"\u003e\n3D \u00b7 WORKS \u2014 E8.0 / CITY-MACHINE MORPHOLOGY + BIOME PROVENANCE\n\nE7.0 is preserved as archaeology rather than active vocabulary. E6's 25 wonders remain preserved and toggleable but default OFF. Canonical payloads remain untouched. The active civilization probe now treats one miniature-sized atomic entity as an entire implied city.\n\nFORM LANGUAGE: hard octagonal brass/stone massing is primary. Selective partial glass shells reveal dense rectilinear urban greeble with strong height variation and function-biased central verticality. Glass is containment/revelation, not the civilization's universal shape. Rings float freely along routes and are anchored only at city interfaces.\n\nSix city-machines test one shared civilization grammar with different verbs: LAUNCHER CITY has an independently rotating matter-accelerator track; DRONE CITY has distributed launch bays and a visible swarm; UNZIP CITY turns one receiving interface into several outgoing ring routes; THUMPER CITY is low/heavy around a dominant reciprocating apparatus; RESERVOIR CITY wraps a hollow visible matter court; LINK CITY is sparse and vertical with multiple network berths. These are visual/mechanical propositions, not replacements for TURRET, THUMPER, DRONE FACTORY, LINK NODE or other canonical payloads.\n\nBIOME PROVENANCE: terrain surface triangles are colored from a persistent world-space biome field (forest/desert/mountain/wetland). Decoration may later be disposable, but provenance is substrate truth. Extruder-produced ordinary matter inherits biome identity and renders with corresponding color while retaining ordinary bearing physics. This is intentionally a small semantic addition, not a resource/crafting taxonomy.\n\nSCALE: footprint remains comparable to existing large payloads; semantic scale increases drastically. Tiny boxes are architectural greeble implying districts/buildings. A moving component can therefore imply a moving district-scale machine. Creeps may now be read as kaiju-scale organisms without changing their existing behavior.\n\u003c/script\u003e\n\u003cscript type=\"text/plain\" id=\"works-inheritance-pass-1\"\u003e\n3D \u00b7 WORKS \u2014 INHERITANCE PASS 1 / QUIET ENTRY STATE\n\nThis distribution build changes presentation defaults, not WORKS mechanics. It opens WORKS on the canonical\nflat signed-density surface with water hidden, the E6 wonder field hidden, and the E8 city-machine morphology\nfield hidden. Canonical payloads, probes, terrain tools, water, wonders, cities, biome provenance, and their\nimplementation remain present and recoverable through the existing controls.\n\nThe purpose is a low-noise first encounter for an inheriting player on a phone. No accessibility interaction\nredesign is claimed yet; observed use should provide evidence before changing the UI or control grammar.\n\u003c/script\u003e\n\u003cscript type=\"text/plain\" id=\"works-e4-terrain-archaeology\"\u003e\n3D \u00b7 WORKS \u2014 E4.1 / TERRAIN SHUFFLE\n\nE4.0 established that repeated local density accretion reads as fast geology rather than life, while\nforcing expensive recurring terrain rebuilds. Continuous growth probes were removed rather than optimized.\n\nWORKS now starts on the canonical flat signed-density table. RESET WORKS restores that flat T0.\n\nSHUFFLE TERRAIN is deliberately discontinuous: one press synthesizes a new varied height field directly\ninto the authoritative signed-density volume, then performs exactly one normal terrain rebuild. Broad\nnoise, finer variation, directional ridges, a basin/highland term, and an isolated mass are randomized\ntogether so repeated presses explore substantially different terrain rather than recoloring one recipe.\n\nThe terrain rewrite uses the same world substrate and rebuild path as all other terrain truth. Therefore\nthe final rendered mesh again authors the retained bearing/drone support field. This is terrain generation,\nnot terrain simulation; there is no ongoing terrain CPU/remesh cost after the button press.\n\u003c/script\u003e\n\u003cscript type=\"text/plain\" id=\"works-e4-sea-archaeology\"\u003e\n3D \u00b7 WORKS \u2014 E4.3 / EDGE OF WORLD\n\nE4.2 proved that a closed translucent sea volume makes randomized negative relief read immediately as\nbounded geography. Inspection exposed z-fighting not at shorelines but at two apparatus coincidences:\nthe water floor shared the plinth-top plane, and the water side walls shared the terrain cut-wall planes.\n\nE4.3 resolves those relationships geometrically rather than with renderer bias. Terrain retains its inset\nWORKS_MATERIAL_APOTHEM boundary. Water now fills outward to WORKS_APPARATUS_APOTHEM, the containing plinth\nedge. Its bottom penetrates the apparatus top by 0.32 world units. Water and terrain therefore no longer\nown coincident boundary faces, and the water floor no longer terminates coplanar with the plinth top.\n\nThe visual relationship is now intentional:\n  terrain/material world \u003c water vessel \u003c apparatus boundary.\nThe sea reaches the literal edge of the represented world while terrain can expose its cut section inside it.\n\nBOUNDED-WORLD DISCOVERY\nThe octagon has earned a useful interpretation without becoming mandatory world ontology: it can contain a\ncoherent piece of world at whatever local scale that piece requires. Continuity need not mean one continuous\nterrain substrate. Separate bounded locations may preserve their own scale and truth while traversal between\nthem is expressed by whatever transport belongs to the experience: portals, ferries, doors, vehicles, dreams,\nor other mechanisms. This is an architectural possibility, not a portal framework or a requirement that every\nlab use bounded geography.\n\nWater remains presentation only: no buoyancy, currents, waves, shoreline logic, underwater state, or machine\nbehavior. RESET WORKS restores flat terrain; SHUFFLE TERRAIN performs one terrain rewrite/rebuild.\n\u003c/script\u003e\n\u003cscript type=\"text/plain\" id=\"works-e4-meteor-archaeology\"\u003e\n3D \u00b7 WORKS \u2014 E4.4 / METEOR COST CLEANUP\n\nMeteors are rare manual tools today but may later become actor-driven orbital bombardment, so their cost should\nscale with event count rather than legacy presentation object count.\n\nInspection found the flight simulation itself cheap: one head, one line trail, interpolation, and one apparatus\nsegment test per meteor. Local crater mutation was already bounded. The legitimate expensive event remains the\nsingle authoritative terrain rebuild at impact.\n\nThe old impact presentation, however, created 34 individual heat meshes, a point light, and 18 more individually\nallocated ember/dust meshes per impact. E4.4 replaces that with two small InstancedMesh batches: one crater heat\nbatch and one rising-mote batch. The point light is removed. Crater/rim deformation, granular impulse, trajectory,\nand impact semantics are unchanged.\n\nThe sea also drops MeshPhysicalMaterial transmission/thickness in favor of a translucent MeshStandardMaterial.\nThe bounded-water read already comes from geometry, opacity, lighting, and terrain occlusion; physical transmission\nwas unnecessary GPU work, especially when bright meteor fragments were composited through it.\n\nThis pass optimizes presentation rather than changing meteor authority. If future actors call sustained orbital\nbombardment, the next evidence should come from actual concurrent-meteor profiling before introducing a larger\nprojectile/FX framework.\n\u003c/script\u003e\n\u003cscript type=\"text/plain\" id=\"works-e4-meteor-bombardment-archaeology\"\u003e\n3D \u00b7 WORKS \u2014 E4.6 / METEOR / METEORS\n\nE4.5 incorrectly made the control itself fire a bombardment and exposed an older sculpt-dispatch bug:\nsculpt() attempted terrainDeformation[kind] even though terrain edits are owned by terrainDeformation.edit().\nE4.6 repairs that boundary and ignores non-sculpt semantic modes defensively.\n\nThe sky tool now behaves as an armed binary choice while preserving the meteor icon:\n  first control tap -\u003e \u2604 METEOR (armed; no strike yet)\n  second control tap -\u003e \u2604 METEORS\n  subsequent taps -\u003e alternate METEOR / METEORS.\nLOOK or another mutually exclusive tool disarms it.\n\nCasting happens by tapping terrain. METEOR calls one full-strength strike at the tapped locus.\nMETEORS calls ten strikes over roughly three seconds with irregular intervals and a small radial aim wander\naround that same tapped locus. The salvo is not map-wide random bombardment.\n\nBombardment meteors carry impact power 0.1. Their crater/rim signed-density mutation is one tenth strength,\nso ten strikes have approximately the terrain-deformation budget of one ordinary meteor while retaining ten\nseparate trajectories, impacts, granular impulses, and visible consequences. This is deliberately not an\norbital-weapons framework; it remains a finite callable world event suitable for later actor invocation.\n\u003c/script\u003e\n\u003cscript type=\"text/plain\" id=\"works-e4-fps-archaeology\"\u003e\n3D \u00b7 WORKS \u2014 E4.7 / VISIBLE FRAME COST\n\nThe decorative bottom note had stopped carrying useful information. It is replaced by a persistent FPS / average\nframe-time readout so performance suspicions can become device evidence while ordinary play continues.\n\nThe meter counts animation frames over a 250 ms window and updates the DOM only at the end of that window. It does\nnot instrument subsystems or attempt GPU timing. The existing meteor profiler remains responsible for impact-phase\nCPU timings. Together they answer different questions: the profiler exposes where an impact spends synchronous CPU\ntime; FPS exposes whether the complete rendered ecology is actually missing frames on the current device.\n\nNo meteor behavior is changed in this pass. E4.6 remains the current meteor semantics until actor-driven bombardment\ncreates evidence worth revisiting.\n\u003c/script\u003e\n\u003cscript type=\"text/plain\" id=\"works-e4-meteor-occlusion-archaeology\"\u003e\n3D \u00b7 WORKS \u2014 E4.9 / IMPACT OCCLUSION\n\nE4.8 briefly tested a fading copy of the old terrain mesh after deformation. It was not harmful, but it was judged\nunnecessary and is not present here. The terrain again changes appearance exactly when its authoritative rebuild occurs.\n\nThe more useful perceptual problem is the meteor's missing impact interval: a bright tracked object reached terrain,\nvanished, and immediately exposed the rebuilt crater. E4.9 adds temporary dirty occlusion instead of animating terrain.\n\nEach impact now erupts nine coarse low-poly smoke/dust puffs from the contact locus. They grow quickly into a dense,\nopaque-ish dirty burst, spread and rise asymmetrically, then loosen and disappear over about one second. The geometry\nis one instanced icosahedron batch and one material per impact. It is intentionally noisy at miniature scale rather\nthan volumetric, physically simulated, or composed from hundreds of particles.\n\nThe crater remains authoritative immediately. Smoke owns no collision, deformation, or simulation truth. Its job is\ncausal presentation: meteor -\u003e obscured violent interval -\u003e revealed consequence. This tests whether the apparent\n'hitch' was partly the eye being asked to witness an instantaneous topology change with no intervening event.\n\u003c/script\u003e\n\u003cscript type=\"text/plain\" id=\"works-e5-weather-deactivated-archaeology\"\u003e\n3D \u00b7 WORKS \u2014 E5.0.2 / PROCEDURAL WEATHER DEACTIVATED\n\nE5.0 tested a persistent shifting cloud layer by reusing the coarse low-poly particulate language discovered in the\nmeteor impact occlusion. The implementation worked after a narrow E5.0.1 initialization-order repair, but the cloud\nlayer did not meet the material/readability bar for persistent world weather.\n\nThe persistent cloud implementation is removed from the active artifact rather than retained as machinery. The useful\nevidence remains: E4.9's temporary impact cloud can read well because it is local, causal, brief, and allowed to be\ncoarse. That does not imply the same geometry/material grammar reads beautifully as distant atmospheric cloud cover.\n\nProcedural weather is intentionally deferred. When revisited, begin from the perceptual/material question\u2014what makes\ncloud, haze, rain, snow, storm mass, or atmospheric depth read beautifully at miniature scale\u2014rather than assuming a\nparticle vocabulary or promoting the impact-cloud implementation.\n\nE4.9 meteor impact occlusion remains active as one successful local use of the coarse particulate mechanism.\n\u003c/script\u003e\n\u003cscript type=\"text/plain\" id=\"works-e5-item-pipe-archaeology\"\u003e\n3D \u00b7 WORKS \u2014 E5.2 / ITEM PIPE\n\nWeather probes are inactive. PIPE END is an active orbital payload. Two landed ends automatically pair in landing\norder: first is intake, second is output. Repeated structural rings frame the route; there is no glass tube.\n\nThe pipe transports the same authoritative discrete bearings already used by WORKS, not inventory counters or\ncosmetic beads. Ordinary bearings and BIOBALLS may therefore share one stream while retaining provenance. Nearby\nunclaimed matter is claimed at the intake, the same physical bearing is constrained along the route, then released\nback into world physics at the output.\n\nThe route consumes the same mesh-authored conservative support field used by bearings and Drone Factory terrain\nnegotiation. Samples along the direct route, plus short look-ahead/look-behind samples, lift the conduit above visible\nterrain. The path periodically rebuilds after deployment, so later terrain changes are negotiated rather than clipped.\n\nEndpoint removal tears down the conduit and spills claimed contents back into world physics. This is intentionally\nnot yet a sorter, branching network, inventory system, recipe graph, or generalized logistics ontology.\n\u003c/script\u003e\n\u003cscript type=\"text/plain\" id=\"works-e5-death-fx-fix-archaeology\"\u003e\n3D \u00b7 WORKS \u2014 E5.2.1 / CREATURE DEATH FX REPAIR\n\nObserved failure: a turret killed a FLOATER EGG and the render loop stopped in updateMeteorFx with\n\"Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'y')\".\n\nThis was not caused by pipe placement or pipe transport. Creature death creates a stationary heatLight impact-FX\nrecord with no velocity vector. updateMeteorFx handled swordLight explicitly but allowed heatLight to fall through\nto the generic moving-particle branch, which assumes f.v exists and dereferences f.v.y. The bug was latent in the\nshared impact-FX dispatcher and can affect CRAWLER, JUMPER, or FLOATER death effects.\n\nRepair is deliberately narrow: heatLight now has its own stationary intensity-fade branch. No pipe behavior,\ncreature durability, bearing burst, meteor behavior, or other FX semantics changed. Visible diagnostics again\nexposed an ownership/type-dispatch mismatch rather than a pipe failure.\n\u003c/script\u003e\n\u003cscript type=\"text/plain\" id=\"world-lab-handoff\"\u003e\nWORLD LAB V0.1 \u2014 RESILIENT CORE / CAPACITY PASS\n================================================\nTHIS PASS OPTIMIZES CAPACITY, NOT METEOR OR SWORD FIDELITY.\nThe laboratory is infrastructure for many grammars. Current meteor/sword implementations are specimens, not privileged architecture.\n\nGRAPHICS FAILURE FINDING\nThe Android/Chrome sad-face WebGL icon means the browser lost the WebGL context; it is not a Three.js UI element. JS error handlers alone cannot catch this class of failure.\n\nV0.1 CHANGES\n- WebGL context loss/restoration is explicitly observed. On loss the animation loop stops and world state remains in CPU memory. On browser restoration, terrain buffers/textures are marked dirty and rendering resumes.\n- RECOVER can rebuild the Three.js renderer after recoverable renderer-loop trouble without resetting world state.\n- Terrain now owns one retained BufferGeometry. Frequent sculpt/remesh operations update reusable DynamicDrawUsage position/normal buffers instead of disposing and replacing a GPU geometry every stroke. Buffers grow geometrically only when required.\n- Terrain normals are generated while copying triangles into the retained buffer; computeVertexNormals no longer scans spare buffer capacity.\n- Baseline shadow maps are disabled. A 2048\u00b2 dynamic shadow map was expensive persistent GPU state that did not define the laboratory's semantics. Future shadows must earn their budget.\n- Device pixel ratio is capped at 1.15 for this mobile-first lab. Resolution is a budget, not world state.\n- RESET now disposes persistent sword geometry/materials instead of merely removing their groups.\n\nCAPACITY RULE\nPrefer retained/poolable resources for high-frequency systems. Persistent semantic objects may own persistent resources. Transient effects should share/pool geometry/material machinery and have bounded populations. Never allocate GPU resources at gesture frequency when an existing buffer can be updated.\n\nRECOVERY RULE\nCPU world state is authoritative. GPU resources are a reconstructible view of it. Context loss should pause presentation, preserve the world, and reconstruct presentation when possible.\n\nWORLD LAB \u2014 V0 SHARED SPATIAL LABORATORY\n========================================\n\nPURPOSE\nThis is the current common home for the user's little-world experiments. Do not treat Living Miniatures, Touch Probes, orbital interactions, terrain deformation, procedural actors, or future perspective experiments as unrelated projects by default. They are disposable/progressive manifestations of one evolving spatial-toy vocabulary.\n\nV0 QUESTION\nCan one persistent volumetric world remain pleasant to inspect and manipulate through multiple observer perspectives while retaining the terrain behaviors and event grammars already earned in earlier probes?\n\nWORLD / PERSPECTIVE / GRAMMAR\nWORLD = substance + discrete objects + processes + accumulated history.\nPERSPECTIVE = observer/camera/interaction lens onto that same world. Changing VIEW must never rebuild, reinterpret, or mutate world state.\nGRAMMAR = a bounded capability that acts on the world: carve, raise, smooth, meteor, embedded sword, and later rip/tether/fling/extrude/rigged actors.\n\nPORTABLE CRAFT KNOWLEDGE \u2014 DO NOT RELEARN\n- Known-good Three.js browser import: https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.180.0/build/three.module.js\n- Three.js is renderer, not ontology.\n- Use visible pre-module window.error + unhandledrejection diagnostics; failures must not go silent.\n- Keep one authoritative runtime/world state. Avoid brittle shadow globals and dual ownership.\n- Scene-specific visual assets are generated/encoded in-artifact. Do not introduce external texture/model/image dependencies casually.\n- Terrain is substance. Geometry is only the current visible boundary. Positive signed density = solid; negative = empty.\n- True caves, tunnels, overhangs, craters, cuts and raised forms are topology of the same substance, not decorative meshes.\n- Camera transforms are observer transforms, not world transforms. Preserve world state across perspective changes.\n- Free inspection is valuable. God-eye panorama is a strong canonical scale, but close inspection is allowed and useful.\n- Detail can be authored above normal viewing resolution; perceptual collapse at miniature scale turns many small assertions into richness.\n- Greebling/appearance/process belong to the semantic object that gives them meaning; avoid a global decoration pass that knows every object type.\n- Directional effects should derive from explicit semantic direction/state, not incidental mesh orientation.\n- Persistent consequences belong to world state; transient FX clarify events and then die.\n- FX resource ownership must be explicit. Shared resources are not disposed by individual effects. Bound sustained transient populations on mobile.\n- Impact residue belongs to the resulting cavity/state, not stale pre-impact surface coordinates. Depth-test it so topology occludes it.\n- A probe is not allowed to redefine the thing it is probing. Promote lessons carefully; preserve local failures as evidence rather than universal law.\n\nPRESERVED DISCOVERIES / ARCHAEOLOGY\nT0.4 STATIC PAINTERLY PROBE: pulled-back miniature scale + dense object-owned geometric evidence produced a dramatic improvement even without fog. Distance is a visual budget, not a prohibition on zoom.\nT0.5 ABYSS: real topology mattered; ecology had to respect cuts; shared boundaries beat independently approximated seams; continuous wall/skirt beat spaghetti terraces.\nT0.7 DEFORMABLE SUBSTANCE: coarse signed-density field + synchronous remesh was fast and responsive on the target phone. This is the foundation used here.\nT0.8.2 METEOR RESIDUE: tiny impacts became fading Dark-Souls-like bonfires when heat/embers lived volumetrically inside the wound.\nT0.8.4.1 FX HARDENING: explicit resource ownership, shared transient geometry and bounded populations survived sustained meteor/sword abuse.\nT0.9\u2013T0.11 SEMANTIC CARVING: noun-to-shape compilation failed; iterative affordance evaluation was richer, but scalar metrics reward-hacked themselves. This branch is preserved knowledge, not the current focus.\nTOUCH T0.1: stable world substance + touch-defined topology + liberated debris + ballistic deposited material was a powerful cheap 2D interaction sketchpad.\nTOUCH SKYHOOK: touch should establish physical relationships between hand and world rather than merely map gestures to UI commands. Successful cheap touch grammars are candidates for promotion here; the brown 2D substrate is not a replacement world architecture.\n\nCURRENT WORLD\nThe current world is intentionally modest: one seeded volumetric landmass rendered from a 30\u00d722\u00d730 signed-density field. It is not the final visual target and not a claim that all future worlds share this resolution or geology.\n\nCURRENT OWNERSHIP MODEL\nWORLD SUBSTRATE owns authoritative terrain/material truth and its deformation realization.\nWORLD SYSTEMS own cross-entity rules such as gravity/support evaluation.\nENTITY CAPABILITIES carry reusable live data such as GravityBody, spatial input/output ports, and semantic local forward. Directional entities author local +Z as forward; behaviors express intent against that entity-level convention rather than inventing mesh-specific signs.\nPAYLOAD BEHAVIORS own machine-specific state and continuous meaning: scan, fire, chew, thump, crawl.\nPRESENTATION may visualize causality (link pulses, sparks, glow) without claiming additional physical simulation.\nPromote machinery only when repeated world evidence makes ownership cross-cutting; capability is not authority.\n\nCURRENT PERSPECTIVES\nVIEW cycles PANORAMA / MINIATURE / CLOSE. They are convenience presets over one camera/world, not separate scenes. LOOK then allows free orbit; two fingers pan/pinch and wheel zoom remains available. Future situated/character perspectives should obey the same invariant: perspective does not redefine world state.\n\nCURRENT TERRAIN OWNERSHIP + VERBS\nThe signed-density terrain substrate owns deformation. Callers request physical changes through terrainDeformation; callers do not own field mutation, remeshing, cache invalidation, or support consequences.\nRAISE adds substance. CARVE subtracts substance. SMOOTH relaxes local density. SURFACE CARVE expresses physical intent from a sampled surface inward and is converted by terrain into a lattice mutation deep enough to cross the visible isosurface reliably.\nHuman sculpting, Extruder bites, meteor craters and payload destruction all enter through this terrain-owned boundary while retaining their own cause-specific shapes/behavior. ONE TERRAIN BITE is currently the shared surface-carve scale used by Extruder chew and Crawler death.\nMETEOR is a persistent mutually exclusive sky-rain grammar that excavates the same field and leaves bounded volumetric fading residue.\nSWORDS is a sibling sky-rain grammar whose blades embed persistently while fire/ember/smoke residue remains ephemeral.\nRESET restores the canonical WORKS T0 octagonal planar field and clears event artifacts. VIEW changes only observation.\n\nTOUCH / INTERACTION THESIS\nDo not start by inventing gestures. Invent physical relationships between hand and world. Cheap probes established useful candidate verbs: RIP, SPRAY, DROP, FLING, TETHER/PULL, JOIN, EXTRUDE/CHANNEL. These are interaction grammars, not a mandatory final control scheme.\nBefore inventing an in-world machine, ask whether the player's hand can simply be that machine.\n\nNEXT RICH AREA\nProcedural articulation: simple rigs with root/body/limb chains/joints/end effectors; behaviors target constraints rather than canned animation names. Deformable worlds especially reward procedural bodies because authored animation assumes authored geometry. A tiny multi-legged actor that genuinely seeks footholds on deformed terrain is a good bounded proof. Do not build a universal character system first.\n\nHANDOFF MAINTENANCE RULE\nThis embedded semantic layer is part of the artifact, not cleanup documentation. Update it whenever implementation changes architecture, ownership, interaction meaning, portable craft knowledge, or an important experimental lesson. Keep portable knowledge distinct from probe-local observations. A fresh model receiving only the latest World Lab artifact should not need to rediscover imports, boot/diagnostic discipline, terrain ontology, camera semantics, or already-paid-for behavior lessons before continuing.\n\u003c/script\u003e\u003cscript type=\"text/plain\" id=\"test-chamber-current-semantics\"\u003e\n3D TEST CHAMBER \